.SS "int libusb_setlocale (const char * locale)"

.PP
Set the language, and only the language, not the encoding! used for translatable libusb messages\&.

.PP
This takes a locale string in the default setlocale format: lang[-region] or lang[_country_region][\&.codeset]\&. Only the lang part of the string is used, and only 2 letter ISO 639-1 codes are accepted for it, such as "de"\&. The optional region, country_region or codeset parts are ignored\&. This means that functions which return translatable strings will NOT honor the specified encoding\&. All strings returned are encoded as UTF-8 strings\&.

.PP
If \fBlibusb_setlocale()\fP is not called, all messages will be in English\&.

.PP
The following functions return translatable strings: \fBlibusb_strerror()\fP\&. Note that the libusb log messages controlled through LIBUSB_OPTION_LOG_LEVEL are not translated, they are always in English\&.

.PP
For POSIX UTF-8 environments if you want libusb to follow the standard locale settings, call libusb_setlocale(setlocale(LC_MESSAGES, NULL)), after your app has done its locale setup\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIlocale\fP locale-string in the form of lang[_country_region][\&.codeset] or lang[-region], where lang is a 2 letter ISO 639-1 code 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
\fBLIBUSB_SUCCESS\fP on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if the locale doesn't meet the requirements 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_FOUND\fP if the requested language is not supported 

.PP
a LIBUSB_ERROR code on other errors 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const char * libusb_strerror (int errcode)"

.PP
Returns a constant string with a short description of the given error code, this description is intended for displaying to the end user and will be in the language set by \fBlibusb_setlocale()\fP\&. The returned string is encoded in UTF-8\&.

.PP
The messages always start with a capital letter and end without any dot\&. The caller must not free() the returned string\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIerrcode\fP the error code whose description is desired 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
a short description of the error code in UTF-8 encoding 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Variable Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "const char* const usbi_locale_supported[] = { 'en', 'nl', 'fr', 'ru', 'de', 'hu' }\fR [static]\fP"

.PP
How to add a new \fBlibusb_strerror()\fP translation: 
.PD 0
.IP "1." 4
Download the latest \fRstrerror\&.c\fP from:
.br
 https://raw.github.com/libusb/libusb/master/libusb/strerror.c  
.IP "2." 4
Open the file in an UTF-8 capable editor  
.IP "3." 4
Add the 2 letter \fRISO 639-1\fP code for your locale at the end of \fRusbi_locale_supported\fP[]
.br
 Eg\&. for Chinese, you would add "zh" so that: 
.PP
.nf
\&.\&.\&. usbi_locale_supported[] = { "en", "nl", "fr" };

.fi
.PP
 becomes: 
.PP
.nf
\&.\&.\&. usbi_locale_supported[] = { "en", "nl", "fr", "zh" };

.fi
.PP
  
.IP "4." 4
Copy the \fR{ / * English (en) * / \&.\&.\&. }\fP section and add it at the end of \fRusbi_localized_errors\fP 
.br
 Eg\&. for Chinese, the last section of \fRusbi_localized_errors\fP could look like: 
.PP
.nf
    }, { / * Chinese (zh) * /
        "Success",
        \&.\&.\&.
        "Other error",
    },
};

.fi
.PP
  
.IP "5." 4
Translate each of the English messages from the section you copied into your language  
.IP "6." 4
Save the file (in UTF-8 format) and send it to \fRlibusb-devel@lists\&.sourceforge\&.net\fP  
.PP

